#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# modular_lambda

#% text
# `modular_lambda(tau)` gives the modular lambda function, `theta_2(0, q)^4/theta_3(0, q)^4` with `q = exp(im*pi*tau)`, for `im_part(tau) > 0`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `lambda(tau)` is the parameter `m` whose nome is `exp(im*pi*tau)` (DLMF 23.15.6); on the imaginary axis it is real and between 0 and 1.

#% item
# It is invariant under `tau -> tau + 2` and under the level-2 congruence subgroup.

#% item
# Machine numbers and balls give numerical values.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
modular_lambda(1.0*im)
#% output
#= 0.5 + 0.0im

#% cell
modular_lambda(0.5 + 1.0*im)
#% output
#= 0.2285655979651146 + 0.6363088584618941im

#% cell
modular_lambda(2.0*im)
#% output
#= 0.029437251522859413 + 0.0im

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The period 2:

#% cell
modular_lambda(0.3 + 0.8*im) - modular_lambda(2.3 + 0.8*im)
#% output
#= 1.1102230246251565e-16 + 3.3306690738754696e-16im

#% text
# The relation to the nome:

#% cell
modular_lambda(1.0*im) - inverse_elliptic_nome_q(exp(-pi*1.0))
#% output
#= -2.220446049250313e-16 + 0.0im

